WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.186
A single box-and-arrow picture rarely answers
every architectural question. A static

00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:10.495
decomposition can show code responsibilities and
dependencies. A runtime view can show

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:15.742
processes and interactions. A deployment view
can show where software executes across

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:16.236
devices.

00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:21.412
State what each box and arrow represents, and
distinguish a code dependency from a runtime

00:00:21.412 --> 00:00:23.540
message or a deployment relationship.

00:00:23.736 --> 00:00:29.175
The 4+1 model organizes complementary views. The
logical view describes key abstractions and

00:00:29.175 --> 00:00:34.259
responsibilities; the process view addresses
runtime concurrency and interactions; the

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:39.638
development view describes code organization;
the physical view maps software onto hardware

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:44.782
or execution nodes. The additional scenarios
connect and help examine the views through

00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:49.866
representative system behavior. For a museum
catalog, a logical view might distinguish

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:55.187
exhibits and collections, a development view
might show modules, and a physical view might

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:57.197
place the application on a server.

00:00:57.236 --> 00:01:02.492
A visitor searching for an exhibit is a scenario
that can be traced through the relevant

00:01:02.492 --> 00:01:07.987
views. These descriptions must agree, but they
need not contain identical boxes or identical

00:01:07.987 --> 00:01:13.482
levels of detail. A maintainer asks which device
runs a process. Would a class diagram alone

00:01:13.482 --> 00:01:18.499
answer the question? Usually not. A physical or
deployment view identifies execution

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:19.096
placement.

00:01:19.236 --> 00:01:24.211
A class diagram describes a different concern
and needs an explicit mapping to runtime and

00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:25.316
deployment elements.

00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:30.455
Architectural descriptions can reuse ideas at
different scopes. A style describes a family

00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:35.536
of structures through constraints on elements
and relationships. A reference architecture

00:01:35.536 --> 00:01:40.389
gives a reusable architectural organization for
a domain. A product-line architecture

00:01:40.389 --> 00:01:44.500
captures shared structure and planned variation
across related products.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.856
These concepts help teams reuse reasoning,
rather than copy a diagram without checking its

00:01:49.856 --> 00:01:50.570
assumptions.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:55.988
C4 provides four nested levels of structural
description: system context, containers,

00:01:55.988 --> 00:02:01.725
components, and code. Context shows the system
in relation to people and external systems.

00:02:01.725 --> 00:02:07.271
Containers show major running applications or
data stores within the system; components

00:02:07.271 --> 00:02:13.072
describe responsibilities within a container;
code shows implementation detail when useful.

00:02:13.072 --> 00:02:17.725
A C4 container is a modeling concept, not
necessarily a Docker container.

00:02:17.725 --> 00:02:23.258
C4 levels and 4+1 views answer different
organizing questions. C4 changes the level of

00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:28.856
detail; 4+1 separates concerns such as code
organization and runtime processes. Neither

00:02:28.856 --> 00:02:34.775
requires every diagram for every audience.
Architectural significance also does not follow
a

00:02:34.775 --> 00:02:40.051
fixed line-count threshold or a rule that
architecture happens only early: revisit

00:02:40.051 --> 00:02:43.010
consequential decisions as the system evolves.

00:02:43.225 --> 00:02:49.013
A context diagram contains every class in the
application. Does the extra detail necessarily

00:02:49.013 --> 00:02:54.613
help its audience? No. A context audience needs
the system boundary, people, and external

00:02:54.613 --> 00:03:00.402
relationships. Class detail can obscure that
purpose; move it to a more detailed description

00:03:00.402 --> 00:03:02.667
when it answers a relevant question.

00:03:02.725 --> 00:03:07.553
An architectural pattern describes a recurring
design problem and an arrangement of

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:12.730
responsibilities that addresses it in a context.
A style constrains the kinds of elements

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:17.675
and relationships allowed in a family of
architectures. The terms can overlap in use;

00:03:17.675 --> 00:03:22.910
explaining the responsibilities, constraints,
and consequences is more useful than relying

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:23.783
only on a name.

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:29.266
The three-tier example separates presentation,
application processing, and data management.

00:03:29.266 --> 00:03:34.689
This helps identify responsibilities and the
interfaces between them. A logical separation

00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:39.992
of these concerns does not by itself prove that
they run on three separate machines. MVC

00:03:39.992 --> 00:03:45.536
separates model, view, and controller
responsibilities around an interface; it should
not be

00:03:45.536 --> 00:03:49.272
assumed to be a one-to-one synonym for three
deployment tiers.

00:03:49.283 --> 00:03:54.443
The sense–compute–control example organizes
observation, computation, and action on an

00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:59.903
environment. It highlights a different set of
responsibilities from a typical presentation-

00:03:59.903 --> 00:04:05.184
and-data application. When selecting any
pattern, state the problem it addresses and the

00:04:05.184 --> 00:04:10.404
constraints it imposes. A named style may
support desired qualities, but its name alone

00:04:10.404 --> 00:04:14.304
cannot guarantee maintainability, performance,
or other outcomes.

00:04:14.304 --> 00:04:19.961
A team labels three boxes MVC. Has it
demonstrated a three-machine deployment? No. MVC

00:04:19.961 --> 00:04:25.421
describes responsibilities. Deployment requires
separate evidence about where those

00:04:25.421 --> 00:04:30.091
responsibilities execute; a diagram label does
not establish placement.
